WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.195 --> 00:00:02.070
YUFENG GUO: There is a
lot of structured data

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.030
out there, perhaps more
than any other type.

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:07.690
And it's ripe for use
with machine learning.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.770
Let's explore a new tool
that aims to automatically

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.560
build and deploy
machine learning models

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.150
on structured data.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.160
Will it live up to the promise?

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.630
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.160
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.730
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.100
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.760
And on this episode,
we're going to try out

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.370
AutoML tables, one of the
newest AutoML products

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.860
that Google has added.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.990
AutoML tables enables you to
automatically build and deploy

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.210
state-of-the-art machine
learning models on structured

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.360
data faster than
you could by hand.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.490
It automates modeling
on a wide range

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.160
of data types from
numbers and classes

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.680
to strings, timestamps,
lists, and nested fields.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.890
What's interesting
about AutoML tables

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.880
is that you can do all of
this without any coding work.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.660
You just take an exported CSV
file, click a few buttons,

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.660
wait a while, and you get
a very highly tuned model

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.920
on the other end.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.500
The team has worked
hard to enable the tool

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.860
to work for a wide variety
of different data types.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.670
And it really explores that
vast space of possible models

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:31.890
and hyperparameters to try to
optimize things on your behalf.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.890
Let's see what using
AutoML tables looks like.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.190
The first step is to import
your training data at the Import

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.310
tab, give it a name,
select the source--

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:45.720
either a table from BigQuery or
a file on Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:47.760
We're going to use
an open dataset that

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.510
describes marketing outcomes
of a Portuguese bank.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.410
And we'll try to model whether
a bank deposit will happen.

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:57.450
This first step
takes a bit of time

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.930
as the system analyzes the
columns of your dataset.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.270
Once it's complete,
you'll get to edit

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.580
the auto-generated
schema and select

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:07.760
the column for predicting on.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.340
Here, you can also update
the column type, as well as

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.040
whether it is nullable or not.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.620
The third tab is called Analyze.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.390
This is a pretty
interesting view.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.060
And I think it will be useful
for those with datasets

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.040
that have a lot
of columns to get

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.120
a nice overview of their data.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.240
You can even click on the
different column names

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.960
to see some statistics
about your columns.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.420
After analyzing the data,
we're ready to do the training.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:38.160
This is where AutoML really
shines because, well, all you

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.860
have to do is click Train.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.140
There are some options
that you can set,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.770
including a maximum
budget of training hours.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.200
This enables you to
experiment with your data

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.350
if you want and limit that
training time before committing

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.757
to a full, longer training run.

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:57.090
You'll notice that the training
times shown are somewhat

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.780
on the long side of things.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.350
And that's because it's
not only doing model tuning

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.005
but also selecting what model
to use in the first place.

00:03:05.005 --> 00:03:06.630
So as a result,
there's a lot of things

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.130
happening during training.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.760
But luckily for us, we
don't have to do anything.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:16.110
Time to just go get lunch
or something, I guess.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.160
And we're back.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.700
Now we're onto the fifth
tab of AutoML Tables, which

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.200
is Evaluation.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.470
If you've used some of the
other AutoML products before,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.975
this will look pretty familiar.

00:03:26.975 --> 00:03:28.350
You can see how
the training did,

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.900
as well as metrics about
the model's performance.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.240
And finally, in tab number
6, we can deploy the model

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:37.570
to get predictions.

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.350
There's even an editor in the
browser that will make requests

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.240
to your endpoint,
so you don't need

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.940
to set up a local environment
to make these calls to try

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.610
and test out your endpoint.

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.490
Notice also, there's an
option at the top of the page

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.580
to toggle between online
predictions via the REST API

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.120
and batch predictions, which
allow you to upload a CSV file

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.570
or point to a BigQuery table
and get prediction results

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:03.360
on that entire file or table.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.210
Considering the amount
of time it takes to use,

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.400
AutoML Tables enables
you to achieve

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.550
a much higher level
of model performance

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.300
than you could reach manually.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.910
So if you have some
structured data

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.130
you're thinking of doing
some machine learning on,

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.070
use this as an excuse
to try out AutoML Tables

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.960
to see if it's useful for you.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.630
By selecting from
state-of-the-art models

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.720
and then carefully tuning
the models during training,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.920
AutoML Tables can potentially
save you a lot of work.

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.150
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.250
And if you enjoyed it,
hit that Like button,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.700
and be sure to subscribe to get
all the latest episodes right

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.780
when they come out.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.290
For now, check out AutoML
tables and harness the insights

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.890
in your structured data.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:51.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

